[{'idx': 0, 'is_supporting': True, 'paragraph_text': 'Universal Pictures motion picture studio. The death in 1930 of Lon Chaney—who throughout the 1920s had made a name for himself by creating grotesque and often painful horror make-ups—opened a niche for Pierce and Universal, Chaney\'s films provided audiences with the deformed, monstrous faces that Pierce and moviegoers of the time enjoyed. Universal\'s first "talkie" horror film, "Dracula" (1931), eschewed elaborate horror make-up. Pierce designed a special color greasepaint for Bela Lugosi for his vampire character, but Lugosi insisted on applying his own make-up. For all film appearances of the character thereafter, Pierce instituted a different look entirely, recasting Dracula', 'title': 'Jack Pierce (make-up artist)'}
