 {'idx': 8, 'is_supporting': False, 'paragraph_text': 'memory used by tmpfs grows and shrinks to accommodate the files it contains and can be swapped out to swap space. Many Unix distributions enable and use tmpfs by default for the /tmp branch of the file system or for shared memory. This can be observed with df as in this example: On some Linux distributions (e.g. Debian, Ubuntu), /tmp is a normal directory, but /dev/shm uses tmpfs. SunOS 4 includes what is most likely the earliest implementation of tmpfs; it first appeared in SunOS 4.0 in late 1987, together with new orthogonal address space management that allowed any object', 'title': 'Tmpfs'}
