 {'idx': 4, 'is_supporting': False, 'paragraph_text': 'K-graph C*-algebra In mathematics, a k-graph (or higher-rank graph, graph of rank k) is a countable category formula_1 with domain and codomain maps formula_2 and formula_3, together with a functor formula_4 which satisfies the following factorisation property: if formula_5 then there are unique formula_6 with formula_7 such that formula_8. Aside from its category theory definition, one can think of k-graphs as higher dimensional analogue of directed graphs (digraphs). k- here signifies the number of "colors" of edges that are involved in the graph. If k=1, k-graph is just a regular directed graph. If k=2, there are two different colors of', 'title': 'K-graph C*-algebra'}
