 {'idx': 12, 'is_supporting': True, 'paragraph_text': 'Canada absorbed some of the developments in American football in an effort to distinguish it from a more rugby-oriented game. In 1903, the Ontario Rugby Football Union adopted the Burnside rules, which implemented the "line of scrimmage" and "down-and-distance" system from American football, among others. Canadian football then implemented the legal forward pass in 1929. American and Canadian football remain different codes, stemming from rule changes that the American side of the border adopted but the Canadian side has not. In the mid-19th century, various traditional football games, referred to collectively as "caid", remained popular in Ireland, especially in County', 'title': 'Football'}
