 {'idx': 10, 'is_supporting': True, 'paragraph_text': 'Soyuz 22 Soyuz 22 (, "Union 22") was a 1976 Soviet manned spaceflight. It was an Earth-sciences mission using a modified Soyuz spacecraft, and was also, some observers speculated, a mission to observe NATO exercises near Norway. The spacecraft was a refurbished Soyuz that had served as a backup for the Apollo-Soyuz Test Project (ASTP) mission the previous year. Cosmonauts Valery Bykovsky and Vladimir Aksyonov spent a week in orbit photographing the surface of the Earth with a specially-built camera. Soyuz 22 was launched to orbit 15 September 1976 at the unusually high inclination of 64.75°, not used since the', 'title': 'Soyuz 22'}
