 {'idx': 27, 'is_supporting': False, 'paragraph_text': 'Breve A breve (, less often ; ; neuter form of the Latin "brevis" “short, brief”) is the diacritic mark ˘, shaped like the bottom half of a circle. As used in Ancient Greek, it is also called vrachy, or brachy. It resembles the caron (the wedge or "háček" in Czech) but is rounded; the caron has a sharp tip. Compare caron: versus breve: The breve sign indicates a short vowel, as opposed to the macron ¯, which indicates long vowels, in academic transcription. It is often used that way in dictionaries and textbooks of Latin, Ancient Greek, Tuareg and', 'title': 'Breve'}]

