 {'idx': 4, 'is_supporting': False, 'paragraph_text': 'Disulfur decafluoride Disulfur decafluoride (SF) is a chemical compound discovered in 1934 by Denbigh and Whytlaw-Gray. Each sulfur atom of the SF molecule is octahedral, and surrounded by five fluorine atoms. SF is highly toxic, with toxicity four times that of phosgene. It was considered a potential chemical warfare pulmonary agent in World War II because it does not produce lacrimation or skin irritation, thus providing little warning of exposure. It is produced by the electrical decomposition of sulfur hexafluoride (SF)—an essentially inert insulator used in high voltage systems such as transmission lines, substations and switchgear. SF is also made', 'title': 'Disulfur decafluoride'}
